# Also test Mimo against another model

There is another reason I wouldn't assume all of this is fundamental Letta overhead.

Letta Code has had recent problems specifically around **reasoning-producing models using OpenAI-compatible provider paths**, including loops where reasoning/tool-call handling causes repeated turns. ([GitHub][8])

And there have been specific compatibility bugs in the `opencode-go` model catalog/tool transport. ([GitHub][9])

So run this matrix:

| Worker          | Tools      | Model                  | Measure         |
| --------------- | ---------- | ---------------------- | --------------- |
| Letta           | none       | Mimo                   | steps / latency |
| Letta           | Write only | Mimo                   | steps / latency |
| Letta           | none       | known-good cheap model | steps / latency |
| Letta           | Write only | known-good cheap model | steps / latency |
| Direct provider | none       | Mimo                   | latency         |

For the tiny task:

```text
Return exactly FAST_OK
```

I expect:

```text
toolset none
≈ one inference
```

If it isn't, **then** something is badly wrong in the local Letta/opencode-go path.

For:

```text
Create result.txt containing FAST_OK
```

full harness will inevitably need tool interaction.

The correct fast test is therefore:

```text
Return this ActionBundle:
{"writes":[{"path":"result.txt","content":"FAST_OK"}]}
```

then let Moltwork write it.

